Key Differences:
Concept	Description	Example (For Set S = {1, 2, 3})
Subset	Any collection of elements from the set (order doesn't matter).	{1, 3}, {2}, {1, 2, 3}, {}, {2, 3}
Power Set	The set of all possible subsets (including the empty set).	{ {}, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3} }
Substring	A contiguous sequence of characters from a string.	"a", "ab", "bc", "abc" (for s = "abc")
Subsequence	A sequence derived by deleting some or no characters, preserving order.	"a", "ab", "bc", "abc", "ac", "" (for s = "abc")
Visual Examples:
Set Example:
Set S = {1, 2, 3}
Subsets: {}, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}
Power Set: { {}, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3} }
String Example:
String s = "abc"
Substrings: "a", "b", "c", "ab", "bc", "abc"
Subsequences: "a", "b", "c", "ab", "ac", "bc", "abc", ""
Conclusion:
Subset and Power Set are used with sets, with a power set being the collection of all subsets.
Substring and Subsequence are concepts for strings, with substrings being contiguous and subsequences maintaining the order but not requiring contiguity.
